Write $a=s+ib$ with real $s,b$, and first translate the real integration variable by $s$. It remains to integrate $e^{-z^2}$ along the horizontal line $\operatorname{Im}z=b$.

For $b>0$, apply the <Cauchy integral theorem> to the rectangle with vertices $-R,R,R+ib,-R+ib$. The <integrand> is an <entire function>, so
$$
\int_{-R}^{R}e^{-x^2}\,dx-\int_{-R}^{R}e^{-(x+ib)^2}\,dx
$$
is the negative of the two vertical-edge integrals. On either vertical edge, $z=\pm R+iy$ and
$$
|e^{-z^2}|=e^{-R^2+y^2}\leq e^{-R^2+b^2}.
$$
Their combined absolute value is at most $2b e^{-R^2+b^2}$ and tends to zero. The rectangle below the real axis gives the same estimate with $|b|$ when $b<0$; $b=0$ needs no deformation. Absolute convergence follows from $|e^{-(x+ib)^2}|=e^{b^2}e^{-x^2}$. Taking $R\to\infty$ proves the <Gaussian contour translation> identity
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-(u+a)^2}\,du=\sqrt\pi,\qquad a\in\mathbb C}.
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $\lambda>0$ and $t>0$, the <positive diffusivity requirement for the forward heat kernel>. The PDF does not explicitly specify the sign of $\lambda$; the stated real <Gaussian function> formula requires this assumption. For a direct calculation, initially assume sufficiently smooth and rapidly decreasing data, so that the <Fourier transform>, its inverse and the following integrations are justified. The resulting <heat-kernel convolution> extends to wider classes of initial data.

Using the stated <Fourier transform> convention and <integration by parts>,
$$
\widetilde{u_{xx}}(k,t)=-k^2\widetilde u(k,t),\qquad
\partial_t\widetilde u=-\lambda k^2\widetilde u.
$$
The transformed <ordinary differential equation> has solution $\widetilde u(k,t)=e^{-\lambda k^2t}\widetilde u_0(k)$. <Fourier inversion> then gives
$$
u(x,t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}u_0(y)K_t(x-y)\,dy,\qquad
K_t(s)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-\lambda tk^2+iks}\,dk.
$$
Complete the square:
$$
-\lambda tk^2+iks
=-\lambda t\left(k-\frac{is}{2\lambda t}\right)^2-\frac{s^2}{4\lambda t}.
$$
Part (a), after scaling by $\sqrt{\lambda t}$, evaluates the remaining <Gaussian integral> even though its shift is imaginary. Therefore
$$
K_t(s)=\frac{e^{-s^2/(4\lambda t)}}{\sqrt{4\pi\lambda t}},\qquad
\boxed{u(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt{4\pi\lambda t}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
e^{-(x-y)^2/(4\lambda t)}u(y,0)\,dy}.
$$
The <heat kernel> has integral one and is an <approximate identity> as $t\downarrow0$, which recovers the initial condition. Mere decay at spatial infinity does not by itself imply every Fourier integral is absolutely convergent; the regularity assumption above supplies a rigorous setting for the derivation.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $s=\lambda t>0$. In the <heat-kernel convolution>, complete the real square as
$$
\alpha^2y^2+\frac{(x-y)^2}{4s}
=\left(\alpha^2+\frac1{4s}\right)
\left(y-\frac{x}{1+4s\alpha^2}\right)^2
+\frac{\alpha^2x^2}{1+4s\alpha^2}.
$$
The <Gaussian integral> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\beta=\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{1+4\lambda t\alpha^2}},\qquad
u(x,t)=\frac{\beta}{\sqrt\pi}e^{-\beta^2x^2}}.
$$
At $t=0$ the same formula gives $\beta=\alpha$. Equivalently, $\beta^{-2}=\alpha^{-2}+4\lambda t$: the <variance> of this normalized <Gaussian function> grows from $1/(2\alpha^2)$ to $1/(2\alpha^2)+2\lambda t$.

The initial profiles have unit integral and concentrate at zero as $\alpha\to\infty$. Precisely, for every smooth compactly supported <test function> $\varphi$,
$$
\int_{\mathbb R}\frac{\alpha}{\sqrt\pi}e^{-\alpha^2y^2}\varphi(y)\,dy
=\frac1{\sqrt\pi}\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-v^2}\varphi(v/\alpha)\,dv
\longrightarrow\varphi(0).
$$
Thus the limit is the <Dirac delta distribution>, rather than an ordinary function: pointwise the profiles tend to zero away from the origin and diverge at the origin. At every fixed $t>0$,
$$
\boxed{u(\cdot,0)\longrightarrow\delta_0\ \text{in distributions},\qquad
u(x,t)\longrightarrow\frac1{\sqrt{4\pi\lambda t}}e^{-x^2/(4\lambda t)}}.
$$
The limiting evolved profile is the <heat kernel> for a unit point source.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

An order-$n$ Cartesian <tensor> has $3^n$ components whose values in two orthonormal Cartesian frames satisfy
$$
T'_{i_1\ldots i_n}=R_{i_1j_1}\cdots R_{i_nj_n}T_{j_1\ldots j_n},\qquad R^TR=I,
$$
with repeated indices summed as in <Einstein notation>. It is an <isotropic tensor> when its components are unchanged by every proper <rotation>, $R\in SO(3)$.

For rank two, a half-turn about any coordinate axis changes the sign of each component having exactly one index along that axis. Invariance therefore makes every off-diagonal component zero. Quarter-turns interchanging two coordinate axes then require $T_{11}=T_{22}=T_{33}$. Thus, without assuming symmetry in advance,
$$
\boxed{T_{ij}=a\delta_{ij}}.
$$
Conversely, the <Kronecker delta> is invariant because $R_{ik}R_{jk}=\delta_{ij}$, so every tensor of this form is isotropic.

For rank three the general proper-rotation invariant form is
$$
\boxed{T_{ijk}=b\epsilon_{ijk}},
$$
where $\epsilon_{ijk}$ is the <Levi-Civita symbol>. The convention matters: the <isotropic rank-three tensor and reflections> distinction says that invariance of a polar <tensor> under all $R\in O(3)$ would force $b=0$. A nonzero $\epsilon_{ijk}$ is invariant under proper <rotations>, and is a <pseudotensor> under the full orthogonal group. The shell integrals below are invariant under reflections as well, so their odd moments vanish.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Introduce the second moment <tensor> $M_{ij}=\int_Vx_ix_j\,d^3x$. The <spherical shell> is preserved by every <rotation>, so this is an <isotropic tensor>, $M_{ij}=A\delta_{ij}$. Its <trace> determines the constant:
$$
3A=M_{ii}=\int_Vr^2\,d^3x
=4\pi\int_{R_1}^{R_2}r^4\,dr
=\frac{4\pi}{5}(R_2^5-R_1^5).
$$
Contracting with the components of the two unit <vectors> gives
$$
\boxed{\int_V(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf n_1)(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf n_2)\,d^3x
=\frac{4\pi}{15}(R_2^5-R_1^5)(\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf n_2)}.
$$
This is the second-order case of <radial tensor moments in three dimensions>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Under inversion $\mathbf x\mapsto-\mathbf x$, the <spherical shell> and its volume element are unchanged, while the product of three <scalar products> changes sign. Its integral is therefore its own negative:
$$
\boxed{\int_V(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf n_1)(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf n_2)
(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf n_3)\,d^3x=0}.
$$
Equivalently, its rank-three moment <tensor> is completely symmetric and cannot have a nonzero multiple of the antisymmetric <Levi-Civita symbol>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $M_{ijkl}=\int_Vx_ix_jx_kx_l\,d^3x$. It is an <isotropic tensor> and is invariant under every permutation of its four indices. Thus the three coefficients in the general rank-four isotropic form must coincide:
$$
M_{ijkl}=C(\delta_{ij}\delta_{kl}+\delta_{ik}\delta_{jl}+\delta_{il}\delta_{jk}).
$$
A double <tensor contraction> gives
$$
15C=M_{iijj}=\int_Vr^4\,d^3x
=4\pi\int_{R_1}^{R_2}r^6\,dr
=\frac{4\pi}{7}(R_2^7-R_1^7).
$$
Here the three contracted <Kronecker delta> products contribute $9$, $3$ and $3$. Contracting instead with the four unit <vectors> yields
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
&\int_V\prod_{a=1}^4(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf n_a)\,d^3x\\
&\quad=\frac{4\pi}{105}(R_2^7-R_1^7)
\big[(\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf n_2)(\mathbf n_3\cdot\mathbf n_4)
+(\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf n_3)(\mathbf n_2\cdot\mathbf n_4)
+(\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf n_4)(\mathbf n_2\cdot\mathbf n_3)\big].
\end{aligned}}
$$
This agrees with subtracting two instances of the <isotropic fourth moment of a ball>.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For an ordinary conservative mechanical system, choose <generalized coordinates> $q_i$ measured from a static <equilibrium point>. Assume the <kinetic energy> is quadratic in velocities and there are no velocity-dependent forces. Expanding about equilibrium, the linear term in the <potential energy> vanishes. Retaining only terms of total degree two gives the <small oscillation> <Lagrangian>
$$
\boxed{L=\frac12\dot{\mathbf q}^{\,T}M\dot{\mathbf q}-\frac12\mathbf q^TK\mathbf q},\qquad
M_{ij}=\left.\frac{\partial^2T}{\partial\dot q_i\partial\dot q_j}\right|_0,\quad
K_{ij}=\left.\frac{\partial^2V}{\partial q_i\partial q_j}\right|_0.
$$
An irrelevant constant has been dropped. Coordinate dependence of $M$ contributes only terms of third or higher total degree. The matrices $M,K$ are symmetric, $M$ is positive definite for independent coordinates, and $K$ is positive semidefinite at a <stable equilibrium>. The <Euler-Lagrange equations> are
$$
\boxed{M\ddot{\mathbf q}+K\mathbf q=0}.
$$
A <normal mode> has a fixed <mode shape> $\mathbf v$ and one time factor: $\mathbf q=\mathbf v Q(t)$. Its <angular frequency> $\omega$ solves the <generalized eigenvalue problem> $K\mathbf v=\omega^2M\mathbf v$, with $\ddot Q+\omega^2Q=0$. Thus $\omega$ is the normal angular frequency, and the frequency in cycles per unit time is $\omega/(2\pi)$. A <zero mode> satisfies $K\mathbf v=0$; its time dependence is $Q=A+Bt$, describing an unopposed displacement or uniform drift rather than a restoring oscillation.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Label the equal <masses> $1,2$ and the mass $2m$ by $3$, in cyclic order. Let their equilibrium angles be $0,2\pi/3,4\pi/3$, with angular displacements $q_1,q_2,q_3$. Because the hoop has unit radius, its three arc lengths are
$$
\ell_1=\frac{2\pi}{3}+q_2-q_1,\quad
\ell_2=\frac{2\pi}{3}+q_3-q_2,\quad
\ell_3=\frac{2\pi}{3}+q_1-q_3.
$$
The <springs> themselves are constrained to the hoop, so these are arc lengths. If their common natural length is $\ell_0$, <Hooke's law> gives $V=\frac k2\sum_i(\ell_i-\ell_0)^2$. Since the arc lengths have fixed sum $2\pi$, all terms linear in the $q_i$ cancel. Dropping the constant,
$$
\boxed{T=\frac m2(\dot q_1^2+\dot q_2^2+2\dot q_3^2),\qquad
V-V_0=\frac k2\big[(q_2-q_1)^2+(q_3-q_2)^2+(q_1-q_3)^2\big]}.
$$
Thus the unspecified common natural length does not affect the motion. The mass and restoring matrices in the <generalized eigenvalue problem> are
$$
M=m\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
K=k\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&-1\\-1&2&-1\\-1&-1&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Exchange of the two equal <masses> separates symmetric and antisymmetric <normal modes>. Direct multiplication gives the complete set
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{mode shape}&\omega^2\\ \hline
(1,1,1)&0\\
(1,-1,0)&3k/m\\
(1,1,-1)&2k/m
\end{array}}.
$$
The first is rigid <rotation> of all three beads: every arc length stays fixed, giving a <zero mode>. In the second, the equal <masses> move oppositely while the heavy bead stays fixed; the net force on that bead cancels. In the third, the equal <masses> move together and the heavy bead moves oppositely with equal angular amplitude. Both nonzero <normal modes> have zero total <angular momentum>. These are the <normal modes of three beads connected by springs on a circular hoop> with $M=2m$.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the <mode shapes> from part (b), and put $\omega=\sqrt{2k/m}$. The initial <velocity> decomposes as
$$
(0,0,\Omega)=\frac\Omega2(1,1,1)-\frac\Omega2(1,1,-1).
$$
There is no antisymmetric component because the initial state treats the equal <masses> identically. Initial displacements are zero, so the <zero mode> contributes a linear drift and the nonzero <normal mode> contributes a sine:
$$
\mathbf q(t)=\frac{\Omega t}{2}(1,1,1)
-\frac{\Omega}{2\omega}\sin(\omega t)(1,1,-1).
$$
In terms of actual angular positions,
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\theta_1(t)&=\frac\Omega2\left(t-\frac{\sin\omega t}{\omega}\right),\\
\theta_2(t)&=\frac{2\pi}{3}+\frac\Omega2\left(t-\frac{\sin\omega t}{\omega}\right),\\
\theta_3(t)&=\frac{4\pi}{3}+\frac\Omega2\left(t+\frac{\sin\omega t}{\omega}\right),\qquad
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{2k}{m}}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The conserved <angular momentum> is initially $2m\Omega$ and the total <moment of inertia> is $4m$, so the common <angular velocity> must be $\Omega/2$, agreeing with the drift. The relative displacements remain of order $\Omega/\omega$, even when the common angle becomes large. Hence small $\Omega/\omega$ preserves the cyclic ordering and small spring extensions at arbitrarily late times. In the ideal arc-spring model the displayed energies are exactly quadratic in the relative angles, so this motion remains exact while that ordering persists.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <group> is a set $G$ equipped with a binary operation satisfying the <group axioms>: closure; <associativity>, $(gh)k=g(hk)$; an <identity element> $e$ with $eg=ge=g$; and an <inverse element> $g^{-1}$ for every $g$, with $gg^{-1}=g^{-1}g=e$.

A <subgroup> $H$ is a subset that is a <group> under the restricted operation. Equivalently, it is nonempty and contains $xy^{-1}$ whenever $x,y\in H$. A <generating set of a group> is a set whose elements and inverses can be multiplied in finite words to produce every group element.

The <group order> is its number of elements, $|G|$. The <order of a group element> $g$ is the least positive integer $r$ with $g^r=e$, or infinity if there is none. <Lagrange's theorem> states that for a finite <group> and <subgroup> $H$, $|H|$ divides $|G|$ and $|G|=[G:H]|H|$. In particular, every finite element order divides the <group order>.

A <cyclic group> is generated by one element, so every element is a power of that generator. A <group isomorphism> is a bijective <group homomorphism>: a bijection $\phi:G\to G'$ satisfying $\phi(gh)=\phi(g)\phi(h)$ for all $g,h$.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By <Lagrange's theorem>, the possible values of the <order of a group element> are
$$
\boxed{1,2,4,8}.
$$
These are possibilities across groups of order eight; they need not all occur in one group.

If every element has order at most two, every element equals its own inverse. Thus for any $x,y$,
$$
xy=(xy)^{-1}=y^{-1}x^{-1}=yx,
$$
so $G$ is an <abelian group>. Choose $a\ne e$, and then $b\notin\langle a\rangle$. The four elements $e,a,b,ab$ are distinct and form a <subgroup> $H$: commutativity and $a^2=b^2=e$ prove closure and inverses. Choose $c\notin H$. The elements of $H$ and $Hc$ are disjoint, since $hc\in H$ would imply $c\in H$. These two four-element sets therefore exhaust $G$.

Every element consequently has a unique expression $a^ib^jc^k$, with $i,j,k\in\{0,1\}$. The map from triples to these products is bijective and respects multiplication by addition of exponents modulo two. Hence
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_2\times C_2\times C_2},
$$
a <direct product of groups> and an <elementary abelian group>.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <cyclic subgroup> $A=\langle a\rangle$ has order four and index two. Every index-two <subgroup> is normal: its complement is both the only nontrivial left <coset> and the only nontrivial right <coset>. Thus <conjugation> by $b$ is an <automorphism> of $A$. It must send its generator $a$ to another element of order four, namely $a$ or $a^3$. Since $b^{-1}=b$,
$$
bab=a\quad\text{or}\quad bab=a^3,
$$
equivalently $ab=ba$ or $ab=ba^3$, as required. Also $b\notin A$, since otherwise $a,b$ could generate at most four elements.

In the commuting case, every word reduces uniquely to $a^jb^\varepsilon$ with $0\leq j<4$ and $\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}$. The eight expressions are distinct because $A$ and $Ab$ are disjoint. Multiplication is addition of the two exponents modulo four and two, respectively. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_4\times C_2}.
$$
This gives the <multiplication table> independently of any other properties of $G$, proving uniqueness up to <group isomorphism>.

In the inversion case, the relation $ba=a^{-1}b$ gives the same eight unique normal forms, now with multiplication
$$
(a^ib^\varepsilon)(a^jb^\eta)
=a^{i+(-1)^\varepsilon j}b^{\varepsilon+\eta},
$$
with exponents reduced modulo four and two. Thus this case also has a uniquely determined <group> structure. A faithful geometric realization is the <dihedral group> of a square, with $a$ a quarter-turn and $b$ a reflection in a line through opposite edge midpoints. For example, on the plane,
$$
a=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
b=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
These preserve the square with vertices $(\pm1,\pm1)$ and satisfy $a^4=b^2=I$, $bab=a^{-1}$. They generate eight distinct transformations, so
$$
\boxed{G\cong D_4\quad\text{(the symmetry group of a square, of order eight)}}.
$$

= d
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**No.** The <quaternion group>
$$
Q_8=\{1,-1,i,-i,j,-j,k,-k\},\qquad
i^2=j^2=k^2=ijk=-1,
$$
is another <non-abelian group> of order eight: for example $ij=k$ while $ji=-k$. Its multiplication comes from the associative <quaternion> algebra, so it does define a <group>.

It is not <isomorphic> to the <dihedral group> of the square. In $Q_8$, only $-1$ has order two; the six elements $\pm i,\pm j,\pm k$ have order four. The square's <dihedral group> has five elements of order two: four reflections and the half-turn. <Group isomorphisms> preserve element orders, so these groups are distinct. The hypothesis in part (c) excludes $Q_8$, since its only element of order two is already in each cyclic subgroup of order four.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <group homomorphism> $f:G\to G'$ satisfies $f(gh)=f(g)f(h)$ for all $g,h\in G$. It consequently sends the <identity element> to the identity and inverses to inverses. Its <group homomorphism kernel> is
$$
\boxed{\ker f=\{g\in G:f(g)=e_{G'}\}}.
$$
A <normal subgroup> $H$ of $G$ is a <subgroup> invariant under <conjugation>:
$$
\boxed{gHg^{-1}=H\quad\text{for every }g\in G}.
$$
Equivalently, $gH=Hg$ for every $g$, so left and right <cosets> coincide.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $M(a,b)=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$. Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
M(a,b)M(c,d)=M(ac,ad+b).
$$
Since $a,c\ne0$, the product is again in $G$. <Associativity> is inherited from <matrix multiplication>, the <identity element> is $M(1,0)$, and
$$
M(a,b)^{-1}=M(a^{-1},-b/a)
$$
lies in $G$. These verify all <group axioms>. This is the <affine group of the complex line>, acting by $z\mapsto az+b$.

For the diagonal subset, $M(a,0)M(c,0)=M(ac,0)$, it contains $M(1,0)$, and $M(a,0)^{-1}=M(a^{-1},0)$. Thus $G'$ is a <subgroup>. Finally,
$$
f(M(a,b)M(c,d))=M(ac,0)
=M(a,0)M(c,0)=f(M(a,b))f(M(c,d)),
$$
so **$f:G\to G'$ is a surjective <group homomorphism>**.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <kernel of a group homomorphism> found in part (b) is the translation subgroup
$$
\boxed{N=\ker f=\left\{T_c=\begin{pmatrix}1&c\\0&1\end{pmatrix}:c\in\mathbb C\right\}}.
$$
It is a <subgroup> because $T_cT_d=T_{c+d}$, $T_0=I$ and $T_c^{-1}=T_{-c}$. It is nontrivial since $T_1\ne I$, and proper since, for example, $M(2,0)\notin N$.

Every kernel is a <normal subgroup>: if $n\in\ker f$, then $f(gng^{-1})=f(g)e f(g)^{-1}=e$. Applying the same argument with $g^{-1}$ gives equality of the conjugate subgroup and the kernel. Here one can also check invariance under <conjugation> directly:
$$
M(a,b)T_cM(a,b)^{-1}=T_{ac}.
$$
Multiplication by nonzero $a$ permutes all $c\in\mathbb C$, so $M(a,b)NM(a,b)^{-1}=N$. Thus $N$ is a nontrivial proper <normal subgroup>, <isomorphic> to the additive <group> $(\mathbb C,+)$.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <symmetric group> $S_n$ consists of all bijections of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, with composition as its operation; its <group order> is $n!$.

An $m$-dimensional complex <group representation> of $G$ is a <group homomorphism> $\rho:G\to GL(V)$, where $V$ is an $m$-dimensional complex <vector space>. After a choice of <basis>, each $\rho(g)$ is an invertible $m\times m$ complex <matrix>, with $\rho(gh)=\rho(g)\rho(h)$.

An <irreducible representation> has $V\ne0$ and no invariant <vector subspace> other than $\{0\}$ and $V$. Two <group representations> $\rho$ on $V$ and $\sigma$ on $W$ are <isomorphic representations> if there exists an invertible <linear map> $S:V\to W$ such that $S\rho(g)=\sigma(g)S$ for every $g$. They are inequivalent if no such map exists; in equal-dimensional <matrix> form this says no fixed change of <basis> conjugates every $\rho(g)$ to $\sigma(g)$.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In a <symmetric group>, two <permutations> are in the same <conjugacy class> exactly when they have the same <cycle type>. For $S_4$ the five possibilities and their sizes are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{cycle type}&\text{representative}&\text{class size}\\ \hline
1^4&e&1\\
2\,1^2&(12)&6\\
2^2&(12)(34)&3\\
3\,1&(123)&8\\
4&(1234)&6
\end{array}}.
$$
There are $\binom42=6$ <transpositions>, three pairings into two unordered pairs, $\binom43(3-1)!=8$ three-cycles, and $(4-1)!=6$ four-cycles. Together with the identity these counts sum to $24=|S_4|$.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The two <one-dimensional representations> are the <trivial representation>, $\rho_+(g)=1$, and the <sign representation>, $\rho_-(g)=\operatorname{sgn}(g)$. The first respects multiplication immediately. For the second, the <sign of a permutation> is the <determinant> of its <permutation matrix>, and multiplicativity of the <determinant> proves $\rho_-(gh)=\rho_-(g)\rho_-(h)$. They are inequivalent since a <transposition> has images $1$ and $-1$, and conjugation by a nonzero scalar cannot change a one-dimensional matrix.

Their <group homomorphism kernels> are
$$
\boxed{\ker\rho_+=S_4,\qquad \ker\rho_-=A_4}.
$$
Both are <subgroups>, indeed <normal subgroups>, because they are kernels. In the second case $A_4$ is the <alternating group> of even <permutations>, with twelve elements.

The <characters> are their scalar values, since the <trace> of a one-dimensional matrix is its only entry. In the class order from part (b),
$$
\chi_+=(1,1,1,1,1),\qquad \chi_-=(1,-1,1,1,-1).
$$
The <character inner product> is the group average of $\chi_+\overline{\chi_-}$, so
$$
\boxed{\langle\chi_+,\chi_-\rangle
=\frac1{24}(1-6+3+8-6)=0}.
$$
Each has squared norm $(1+6+3+8+6)/24=1$, in agreement with <character orthogonality> for distinct <irreducible representations>.

= d
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For a finite <group> over $\mathbb C$, the number of inequivalent <irreducible representations> is its number of <conjugacy classes>, and the <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions> equals the <group order>. There are therefore five irreducibles for $S_4$, with squared dimensions summing to $24$.

First establish that part (c) found every <one-dimensional representation>. In such a representation, conjugate elements have the same scalar image. All <transpositions> are conjugate and square to the identity, so they all have one common image $s$ with $s^2=1$. <Transpositions> generate $S_4$, hence $s=1$ gives exactly the <trivial representation>, while $s=-1$ gives exactly the <sign representation>. There are precisely two degree-one irreducibles.

The other three dimensions $d_1,d_2,d_3$ are integers at least two and satisfy
$$
d_1^2+d_2^2+d_3^2=24-1-1=22.
$$
No one of them can be at least four, since $4^2+2^2+2^2=24>22$. They are therefore all two or three. Starting from three twos gives sum of squares $12$; each replacement of a two by a three adds $5$. To reach $22$ requires exactly two replacements. Thus the complete list is
$$
\boxed{1,\ 1,\ 2,\ 3,\ 3}.
$$
The count of <conjugacy classes> guarantees that these are all the inequivalent complex <irreducible representations>, not just dimensions of a proposed incomplete list.
